Amazon Route 53 is a scalable and highly available Domain Name System (DNS) web service offered by AWS. It can perform a 
variety of DNS-related tasks, as well as domain registration and health checks. 
Here's a comprehensive list of what Route 53 can do:

🌐 1. Domain Registration
Register new domain names (e.g., .com, .org, .tech)
Transfer domains between registrars
Manage domain WHOIS contact information
-----------------------------------------
🧭 2. DNS Service (Hosted Zones)
Create and manage hosted zones for domains
Create various DNS record types:
A (IPv4 address)
AAAA (IPv6 address)
CNAME (Canonical name)
MX (Mail exchange)
TXT (Text for SPF, DKIM, etc.)
SRV (Service locator)
NS (Name server)
PTR (Reverse DNS)
Alias (specific to AWS services)
------------------------------------------------------
⚖️ 3. Traffic Routing Policies
Route 53 supports advanced traffic routing using policies:
Simple routing – standard one-record response
Weighted routing – split traffic across multiple resources by percentage
Latency-based routing – direct users to the lowest-latency endpoint
Failover routing – route to healthy endpoints with active-passive setup
Geolocation routing – route based on user's location
Geoproximity routing (with Route 53 Traffic Flow) – based on geography and bias
Multivalue answer routing – return multiple healthy IPs for DNS-based load balancing
-------------------------------------------------------------
❤️‍🩹 4. Health Checks and Monitoring
Monitor the health of web servers or endpoints (HTTP, HTTPS, TCP)
Integrate with CloudWatch for alarms
Use health check status in routing decisions (e.g., failover)

🩺3. How Does Route 53 Monitor Health & Redirect Traffic?
Route 53 has built-in health checks:

You configure HTTP, HTTPS, or TCP checks on a public endpoint.
You define what status code indicates health (e.g., 200 OK).
Health checks can be associated with:
Individual DNS records
ELBs, EC2 IPs, on-prem servers, etc.

If an endpoint becomes unhealthy:
Route 53 will stop including that endpoint’s IP in DNS responses.
Works with failover routing, multivalue routing, or simple routing with health checks.
-----------------------------------------------------------------
🗺️ 5. Domain Name Resolution for Internal AWS
Works with Amazon VPC to create private hosted zones
DNS resolution within VPCs for internal services
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔐 6. DNS Security and Compliance
DNSSEC for domain name registration (signing is not yet supported for hosted zones)
IAM policies to control access to Route 53 resources
Logs DNS queries using Route 53 Resolver Query Logging
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🧰 7. Integration with Other AWS Services
Elastic Load Balancer (ELB)
CloudFront
S3 Static Website Hosting
API Gateway
AWS Global Accelerator
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚙️ 8. Management and Automation
Fully API-driven (can use CLI, SDKs, or AWS Management Console)
Route 53 Traffic Flow visual editor for managing complex routing
Support for Infrastructure as Code via CloudFormation, Terraform, etc
