Q. How Route 53 Differs from a Load Balancer
Route 53 operates at the DNS level, deciding where to send users before a connection is made. It doesn’t actually handle the 
user’s traffic. A load balancer, like AWS ELB, works at the network or application level, handling live traffic and distributing
it among backend servers. In simple terms, Route 53 tells the client "connect to this IP", while a load balancer says "you’re
connected, now let me pass your request to a healthy server".
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Feature                   | **Amazon Route 53**                                         | **Elastic Load Balancer (ELB)**                      |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| **Type**                  | DNS-based routing (Layer 3)                                 | Application/Network layer load balancing (Layer 4/7) |
| **Scope**                 | Global (DNS level)                                          | Regional (within AWS region)                         |
| **Traffic Decision Time** | Before connection is made                                   | After client connects                                |
| **Function**              | Chooses *which server/region/IP* a client should connect to | Balances traffic among instances within a region     |
| **Response**              | Sends a DNS response with IP address                        | Distributes actual TCP/HTTP requests to instances    |
| **Scaling**               | Passive (no autoscaling)                                    | Actively integrates with Auto Scaling                |
| **Health Checks**         | External URL/IP health checks at DNS level                  | Internal instance health monitoring                  |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q.. How Route 53 Handles Traffic Scaling
ANs: Route 53 itself doesn’t scale your application; instead, it scales DNS queries. It’s capable of handling millions of DNS 
requests per second. If you want to scale your actual application servers, you still need to use services like EC2 Auto Scaling 
or Elastic Load Balancing. Route 53 helps by routing users to the best available region, server, or service based on routing
policies.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Load Balancers: Distribute traffic within a single region across server replicas. They don’t care about user location — only about balancing load across instances.
Route 53 Geolocation: Sends users to different regions or endpoints based on where they are in the world. You define the rules.
Route 53 Latency: Picks the region or endpoint that gives the fastest response time, automatically. It adapts to network performance.
CloudFront (Edge): Serves cached content from edge servers closest to the user, drastically reducing latency for static content.
